<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">East African Rift</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Great Rift Valley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The East African Rift is a system of rift valleys running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, part of what is often called the Great Rift Valley. Its floor holds a chain of lakes and fertile volcanic soils. In Ethiopia it separated highland kingdoms such as Wolaita from their neighbours, and in Kenya it divided the coast from the western highlands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-african-rift.jpg" alt="East African Rift" width="320" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: USGS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tectonical_map_of_East_Africa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The East African Rift is an active continental rift zone that began developing around the start of the Miocene, 22 to 25 million years ago. It is a narrow zone along which the African plate is splitting into two plates, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> and the Nubian, at 8 to 9 millimetres a year. Many of the African Great Lakes lie within the rift valley.</p><p>North of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> triple junction the rift follows two paths, one west to the Red Sea Rift and one east to the Aden Ridge in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a>. To the south it has two main branches. The Eastern Rift Valley, also called the Gregory Rift, includes the Main Ethiopian Rift and the Kenyan Rift Valley and continues into northern Tanzania. The Western Rift Valley includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-rift">Albertine Rift</a> and continues through Tanzania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, the valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the rift formed, enormous continental flood basalts erupted and uplifted the Ethiopian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> and East African plateaus. Most of the volcanism of the Oligocene coincided with the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a> about 30 million years ago. In the first stage of rifting, magmatism and faulting were localised along the whole zone. In the second stage the large boundary faults became inactive and magmatic activity concentrated within the rifts.</p><p>Explanations of the rift have changed over time. In 1972 it was proposed that differences in crustal density, not tectonic activity, caused it. Since the 1990s evidence has been found for mantle plumes beneath the region, and some proposed an African superplume. The view put forward in 2009 holds that magmatism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plate-tectonics">plate tectonics</a> feed back on one another under oblique rifting. According to the marine geologist Kathleen Crane, the rift could eventually separate eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from the mainland, an event that might take tens of millions of years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The rift zone contains active and dormant volcanoes, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kilimanjaro">Mount Kilimanjaro</a>, Mount Kenya, Mount Nyiragongo, Erta Ale and Ol Doinyo Lengai. Erta Ale, in the Afar Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, has been active continuously since at least 1967, and Dalafilla's eruption of 2008 is the largest recorded in Ethiopian history. The rift is the largest seismically active rift system on Earth, and most earthquakes occur near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar-triangle">Afar Depression</a>.</p><p>The valley has also been a rich source of hominid fossils, because sediments from the eroding highlands filled it quickly and preserved remains. They include Lucy, a partial australopithecine skeleton found by Donald Johanson and more than 3 million years old, and work by Richard and Mary Leakey.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “East African Rift” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During World War II English Christian missionaries visited the Kipsigis people of the <b>Great Rift Valley</b> of Kenya, bringing a wind-up gramophone and records of the American country singer Jimmie Rodgers.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chemirocha">Chemirocha</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1895 the British government took over the territory between the coast and the <b>Rift Valley</b> from the Imperial British East Africa Company.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-africa-protectorate">East Africa Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom lay between the Omo river and the <b>Rift Valley</b> lakes, south of the Gurage and Hadiya lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-wolaita">Kingdom of Wolaita</a></li></ul></section>
